### Extend GraphQL Context with a Function

Source: https://github.com/graphql-hive/graphql-yoga/blob/main/website/src/content/docs/features/context.mdx

Use a function for the `context` property in `createYoga` to dynamically generate context for each request. The function receives the initial context and its return value is merged.

```typescript
import { createServer } from 'http'
import { createSchema, createYoga } from 'graphql-yoga'

const yoga = createYoga({
  schema: createSchema({
    typeDefs: /* GraphQL */ `
      type Query {
        someNumber: Int!
      }
    `,
    resolvers: {
      Query: {
        someNumber(_, _args, context) {
          return context.someNumber
        }
      }
    }
  }),
  context() {
    return { someNumber: 13 }
  }
})

const server = createServer(yoga)
server.listen(4000, () => {
  console.info('Server is running on http://localhost:4000/graphql')
})
```

--------------------------------

### Initialize GraphQL Yoga Server

Source: https://github.com/graphql-hive/graphql-yoga/blob/main/README.md

Create a schema and start a Node.js HTTP server using the Yoga instance.

```ts
import { createServer } from 'node:http'
import { createSchema, createYoga } from 'graphql-yoga'

const yoga = createYoga({
  schema: createSchema({
    typeDefs: /* GraphQL */ `
      type Query {
        hello: String
      }
    `,
    resolvers: {
      Query: {
        hello: () => 'Hello from Yoga!'
      }
    }
  })
})

const server = createServer(yoga)

server.listen(4000, () => {
  console.info('Server is running on http://localhost:4000/graphql')
})
```

--------------------------------

### Build an Executor

Source: https://github.com/graphql-hive/graphql-yoga/blob/main/website/src/content/docs/features/testing.mdx

Initialize an HTTP executor using the yoga.fetch method to handle GraphQL operations.

```ts
import { createSchema, createYoga } from 'graphql-yoga'
import { buildHTTPExecutor } from '@graphql-tools/executor-http'

const schema = createSchema({
  typeDefs: /* GraphQL */ `
    type Query {
      greetings: String!
    }
  `,
  resolvers: {
    Query: {
      greetings: () => 'Hello World!'
    }
  }
})

const yoga = createYoga({ schema })

const executor = buildHTTPExecutor({
  fetch: yoga.fetch
})
```

--------------------------------

### GraphQL Yoga v3 Context Example

Source: https://github.com/graphql-hive/graphql-yoga/blob/main/website/src/content/docs/migration/migration-from-yoga-v1.mdx

Shows context setup in GraphQL Yoga v3 using createYoga, accessing request headers via the Fetch API Request object.

```typescript
import { createServer } from 'http'
import { createSchema, createYoga } from 'graphql-yoga'
import { db } from './db'
import { resolvers, typeDefs } from './schema'

const yoga = createYoga({
  schema: createSchema({ typeDefs, resolvers }),
  context(initialContext) {
    const authHeader = initialContext.request.headers.get('authorization') ?? null

    return { db, authHeader }
  }
})

const server = createServer(yoga)
server.listen(4000, () => {
  console.info('Server is running on http://localhost:4000/graphql')
})
```

--------------------------------

### Define User Context Factory Function

Source: https://github.com/graphql-hive/graphql-yoga/blob/main/website/src/content/docs/features/context.mdx

Use a factory function with the `context` parameter in `createYoga` to define a custom user context. This function receives the request and can extract custom headers to be included in the context.

```typescript
import { createServer } from 'http'
import { createYoga } from 'graphql-yoga'

const yoga = createYoga({
  context: async ({ request }) => {
    // get custom header value
    const foo = request.headers.get('x-foo') ?? null
    return { foo }
  }
})

const server = createServer(yoga)
server.listen(4000, () => {
  console.info('Server is running on http://localhost:4000/graphql')
})
```

